      "urpx:hasCoverageClaim": {
        "@id": "gpc:cc-slm-2026-06-01",
        "@type": "urpx:CoverageClaim",
        "urpx:completenessResult": {
          "@id": "urpx:partial"
        },
        "urpx:selfContained": true,
        "urpx:explainedBy": {
          "@type": "urpx:Evaluation",
          "@id": "gpc:cc-slm-2026-06-01-evaluation",
          "urpx:evaluationKind": {
            "@id": "urpx:coverageEvaluation"
          },
          "urpx:description": "This filing does not carry everything its source document states. 8 parts are deferred, listed here in the same words as the filing's tariff.toml [modeling].deferrals so the two records cannot drift. (1) Hours-use bands. The sheet prices kWh in bands bounded by hours times the Billing Demand: not greater than 150 hours (in blocks of kWh), 150 to 300 hours, 300 to 500 hours and over 500 hours. A urpx:ServiceBracket bound is a fixed number (https://github.com/urpx-org/urpx/issues/87), so each band's kWh is a calculated MetricInput whose formula is text in its CalculationMethod, and the first band carries the kWh blocks; a consumer has to split the month's kWh by the Billing Demand. clj-urpx sums every energy ledger for a marginal rate, and the rider subtotals sum the same prices, so this filing's recorded marginal rate adds each higher band's price to the first block's, where the sheet prices a month's first kWh at the first block's price alone. (2) Billing Demand. Billing Demand is a calculated MetricInput on the 30-minute kW demand, with the sheet's ratchets over the current month and the preceding eleven, its floors and its rule for a customer changing from a time of use tariff stated in its description and formula. URPX v0.5.1 does not evaluate a calculationFormula, so a consumer has to compute it from twelve months of demand history, contract capacity and contract minimum. The sheet's seasons for it are billing months. (3) Fuel seasons by billing month. FCR-27 prices June through September and October through May, which Schedule R's bills show is read by the billing month a bill is rendered in. The JSON-LD gates the fuel PriceDefinitions on calendar months, so usage in the weeks a billing period runs ahead of its billing month takes the other season's fuel price. (4) Voltage level and franchise fee location. FCR-27's three voltage levels and MFF-11's two locations are crossed in one ProfileAlternatives set of six alternatives, because URPX v0.5.1 allows one set per PlanElements and the franchise fee's energy subtotal must include that voltage level's fuel prices. A consumer selects one alternative, for example Secondary Distribution inside city limits. (5) Municipal Franchise Fee base. MFF-11 applies to the total revenues of each bill, which include the ECCR-15 and DSM-C-15 amounts. Its multiplier Prices here are based on the energy-and-fuel subtotal and on the non-energy charges' Prices, not on the ECCR and DSM-C multiplier Prices, so the franchise fee on those two amounts, about 0.44 percent of the base bill inside city limits, is not carried. (6) Excess Reactive Demand. Where a power factor below 95 percent lagging is indicated, the Company may install metering for reactive demand and bills kVAR above one-third of the measured kW at $0.43 per kVAR. The JSON-LD carries the $0.43 on a calculated MetricInput whose formula is text in its CalculationMethod; a consumer has to compute the excess kVAR, and supplies none for a customer the Company does not meter for it. (7) Minimum Monthly Bill. The sheet sets it at the $119.97 Basic Service Charge plus $15.07 per kW of Billing Demand in excess of 30 kW, plus excess kVAR charges, ECCR, the Demand Side Management Commercial Schedule, Fuel Cost Recovery on the current month's kWh and the Municipal Franchise Fee. A minimum is a floor under the bill, which no Price expresses, so it is not carried; a modeled bill below it is low by the difference. (8) Metering and Term of Contract. The customer may be responsible for any additional cost of installing and removing equipment required to meter or deliver the service, and the term is five years. Neither is priced on the sheet and neither is carried.",
          "urpx:evaluatedOn": "2026-10-19T00:00:00"
        }
      }
    }
  }
